/* The program defines its own rule named Copy, which is not a tree copier:
   it returns e whatever it is given.  Apply passes its input x, a list of
   a and b (q0), to f, whose type demands a list of c and d (q1), so the
   answer is unsat (as in higher_order_argument_wrong_state_unsat, which
   differs only in its Copy).  The reduction checks the argument given to
   f, and builds f's result, with a tree copier of its own; when that
   copier was named Copy, the program's rule replaced it, every argument
   check read e, which q1 accepts, and the answer was sat. */
%BEGING
Apply f x -> f x.
Copy x -> e.
%ENDG

%BEGINA
q0 a -> q0.
q0 b -> q0.
q0 e -> .
q1 c -> q1.
q1 d -> q1.
q1 e -> .
%ENDA

%BEGINT
Apply : (q1 -> q1) -> q0 -> q1.
%ENDT
